1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:02,300
 If you remember at the beginning of the lesson, I said

2
00:00:02,300 --> 00:00:06,340
 the Manifest is what keeps an Android application together. So

3
00:00:06,340 --> 00:00:08,780
 what is a Manifest exactly? In a Manifest is a

4
00:00:08,780 --> 00:00:12,670
 XML file that declares, among other things, all of the steady

5
00:00:12,670 --> 00:00:17,730
 components of your app. Activities, services and content providers. Broadcast

6
00:00:17,730 --> 00:00:20,535
 receivers could be defined in either statically in the Manifest or

7
00:00:20,535 --> 00:00:23,210
 dynamically in run time. So they don't necessarily have to

8
00:00:23,210 --> 00:00:25,840
 be in the Manifest, but they might be. The second thing.

9
00:00:25,840 --> 00:00:28,830
 That the manifest declares are all the permissions required

10
00:00:28,830 --> 00:00:31,860
 for your app to work. For example, if the application

11
00:00:31,860 --> 00:00:35,760
 requires network access or access to calling and messaging capabilities,

12
00:00:35,760 --> 00:00:39,440
 that must be specified in the manifest. The manifest also

13
00:00:39,440 --> 00:00:42,710
 specifies the entry point for the application. That is,

14
00:00:42,710 --> 00:00:46,490
 which activity should be launched when the application is executed.

15
00:00:46,490 --> 00:00:48,530
 So what that means is that when you click on

16
00:00:48,530 --> 00:00:51,100
 an app to start the app up the system will

17
00:00:51,100 --> 00:00:53,740
 look into the Manifest to see which activity has to be

18
00:00:53,740 --> 00:00:56,410
 started as a consequence of that. So which one is the

19
00:00:56,410 --> 00:00:59,559
 entry point for your application as we were saying. The Manifest

20
00:00:59,559 --> 00:01:02,490
 also declares the version of the app, and this is used for

21
00:01:02,490 --> 00:01:05,590
 updates. Because the system is able to check which version you

22
00:01:05,590 --> 00:01:09,300
 have and which one is the latest version available for that application.

23
00:01:09,300 --> 00:01:13,520
 And finally, the manifest also declares the lowest Android SDK version

24
00:01:13,520 --> 00:01:16,200
 for which is app is valid. So this is something that is

25
00:01:16,200 --> 00:01:20,220
 checked at installation time to make sure that the application can run on

26
00:01:20,220 --> 00:01:22,250
 your version of the operating system. So

27
00:01:22,250 --> 00:01:24,690
 again, nothing too exciting, but information that

28
00:01:24,690 --> 00:01:26,930
 is necessary for the system to understand

29
00:01:26,930 --> 00:01:29,080
 how your application is structured and what

30
00:01:29,080 --> 00:01:30,520
 the different parts are and what is

31
00:01:30,520 --> 00:01:30,520
 needed for the application to operate correctly.

